  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=\u27e82(t-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t),\\,2(t-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)\u27e9$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$",
  "worked_solution": "At the point where $t=\\pi $,  the vector ${r}^{\u2032}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)],\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)]\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82(1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)\u27e9 \\\\ {r}^{\u2032}(\\pi ) & =\u27e82(1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi ),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi )\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82(1-(-1)),\\,2(1+0)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84,\\,2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $  is  $\u27e84,\\,2\u27e9$",
